    "text": "Let E be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with Borel measurable structure and canonical Lebesgue volume, permitting dimension zero. The approved ambient section instances are part of the contract. Let W:E->R be C1 and assume exp(-W) is Lebesgue integrable. Define mu=volume.tilted(x->-W(x)), i.e. the measure with density exp(-W)/integral exp(-W) relative to volume; positive normalization is not built into that definition.\nFor every real-linear partial operator D:L2(mu;R)->L2(mu;E), assume D is closable and its graph is exactly characterized as follows: for every scalar L2 class a and vector L2 class H,\n  (a,H) belongs to graph(D) if and only if there exists a smooth compactly supported phi:E->R with a=phi mu-almost everywhere and H=gradient(phi) mu-almost everywhere.\nD.closure is the operator with that same topological graph closure, on its submodule domain. This D is fixed before epsilon and the forcing f.\nFor every real epsilon>0 and every f in L2(mu;R), there exists u in D.closure.domain satisfying all conclusions below simultaneously. Write u_rep and f_rep for the standard measurable representatives of the actual scalar L2 classes, and G_rep for the standard representative of G=D.closure(u) in L2(mu;E). Abbreviate the exact scalar expression by\n  r(x)=epsilon*u_rep(x)+<gradient(W)(x),G_rep(x)>_E-f_rep(x).\n(a) For every z in D.closure.domain,\n  epsilon*<u,z>_{L2(mu;R)}+<D.closure(u),D.closure(z)>_{L2(mu;E)}=<f,z>_{L2(mu;R)}.\n(b) u_rep and G_rep are locally integrable with respect to Lebesgue volume.\n(c) For every C1 compactly supported psi:E->R and every direction a in E, both x->psi(x)*<G_rep(x),a>_E and x->u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x a) are Lebesgue integrable, and\n  integral psi(x)*<G_rep(x),a>_E dx = - integral u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x a) dx.\n(d) The real scalar function x->||r(x)||^2 is locally Lebesgue integrable.\n(e) For every compact set K in E, u_rep, G_rep and r each satisfy MemLp at exponent 2 with respect to volume.restrict K, including a.e. strong measurability and finite L2 norm for that actual restricted measure.\n(f) For every C2 compactly supported phi:E->R, the two functions x->u_rep(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) and x->r(x)*phi(x) are Lebesgue integrable and\n  integral u_rep(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) dx = integral r(x)*phi(x) dx.\n(g) For every C2 compactly supported chi:E->R, define the following actual functions, with the notation v_chi corresponding to the let-defined v in the statement:\n  v_chi(x)=chi(x)*u_rep(x),\n  G_chi(x)=chi(x) scalar-multiplied by G_rep(x) + u_rep(x) scalar-multiplied by gradient(chi)(x),\n  F_chi(x)=chi(x)*r(x)+2*<gradient(chi)(x),G_rep(x)>_E+u_rep(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(chi)(x).\nThen v_chi, G_chi and F_chi each satisfy MemLp at exponent 2 with respect to the full volume measure, with actual a.e. strong measurability and finite global L2 norm. All three topological supports are contained in the topological support of chi:\n  tsupport(v_chi) subset tsupport(chi),\n  tsupport(G_chi) subset tsupport(chi),\n  tsupport(F_chi) subset tsupport(chi).\nFor every C1 compactly supported psi:E->R and every a in E, both x->psi(x)*<G_chi(x),a>_E and x->v_chi(x)*(fderiv R psi x a) are Lebesgue integrable, and\n  integral psi(x)*<G_chi(x),a>_E dx = - integral v_chi(x)*(fderiv R psi x a) dx.\nFor every C2 compactly supported phi:E->R, both x->v_chi(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) and x->F_chi(x)*phi(x) are Lebesgue integrable, and\n  integral v_chi(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) dx = integral F_chi(x)*phi(x) dx.\nAll unannotated local-integrability, integrability and integral assertions use canonical Lebesgue volume. The Hilbert inner products use the actual law mu. Laplacian.laplacian on chi and phi is the actual finite-basis test-function trace; no classical Laplacian of the arbitrary representative u_rep or v_chi is asserted. The same u and G serve every test and every cutoff. chi is any real C2 compact-support function, without positivity, value-range or plateau assumptions. The localized functions are given by these three formulas, not independent existential choices; no membership of v_chi in a weighted partial-operator domain is asserted.\nThe result is a universal implication for any such supplied D; it does not add a dense-domain premise or assert existence of D. One u is selected per epsilon,f before all compact restrictions, ordinary tests and cutoffs. No solution uniqueness, H2 regularity, classical Delta u, core characterization, measurable parameter selection or quantitative bound is an explicit conclusion.",
